* Passive and discrete component models for OPA1641 mic simulation
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

* 1N4148 small signal diode
* Parameters from ON Semiconductor / Vishay published SPICE data
.model 1N4148 D (Is=2.52n N=1.752 Rs=0.568 Ikf=0 Xti=3 Eg=1.11
+ Cj0=4p M=0.4 Vj=0.75 Fc=0.5 Isr=1.48n Nr=2 Bv=100 Ibv=100u Tt=11.54n)

* BZX55C56 — 56V zener diode
* Vz=56V at Iz=5mA, Zz~70Ω
.model BZX55C56 D (Is=1n N=1 Rs=10 Cj0=50p Bv=56 Ibv=5m Vj=0.6 M=0.4)

* NTE10/3 transformer model (used reversed: the tap section as primary)
* The data sheet gives the turns as 1:3:10 and a response figure, and nothing else: no inductance, no
* winding resistance, no loss. The values here are measured on the part on its own (one unit, #137,
* docs/transformer.md):
*   Turns, output : tap : full = 1 : 3.58 : 10.03. The full winding agrees with the data sheet, the tap
*     does not. Told from a core loss by using two series resistors (1.2k and 4.7k): the level follows
*     3.58 without loss within 0.2 dB with both, where a ratio of 3 with a loss would be 2.2 dB lower with
*     4.7k. It accounts for the whole microphone measuring the tap wiring 8.8 dB above the full winding
*     (10:3 gives 10.0, 10.03:3.58 gives 8.9).
*   DC resistance: red-blue (tap) 527Ω, red-black (full) 1658Ω, white-yellow (output) 41.8Ω.
*     Two other units: red-blue 521 and 540Ω, red-black 1720Ω, white-yellow 42Ω.
*   Magnetising branch across the tap section: 17.9H in series with (8.5H parallel 2.23kΩ), fitted to the
*     response with the output side open behind 1.2k, 10 Hz to 5 kHz, small level (rms error 0.06 dB).
*     One inductance does not fit (22H, 0.2 dB): below 100 Hz the response falls more slowly than one
*     pole. Not fitted, and within 0.25 dB rms from 20 Hz to 20 kHz with these values: the response into
*     1.2kΩ, behind 4.7k, and of the full winding. Level dependence is not modelled.
*   No core loss resistor: none shows in the levels (the 3k that stood here was inferred from the 8.8 dB
*     above, which the ratio explains).
*   Leakage inductance 10mH on the tap side, as before. Not measured: with the output side open the
*     response is flat within 0.2 dB to 40 kHz.
* OPEN: in the whole microphone the low end below 50 Hz does not follow this branch (measured -5.0 dB at
*   20 Hz and -0.6 dB at 30 Hz re 1 kHz on the tap wiring, #128; this model gives about -1.9 and -1.8
*   there). The part was measured with no direct current in any winding; the reason is not known.
* The ideal transformer is a pair of controlled sources, so the ratio is exact and the measured branch
*   carries the low-frequency behaviour.
.subckt NTE10_3 PRI_A PRI_B SEC_P SEC_N
Rp1    PRI_A  P1     527
Lleak  P1     P2     10m
Lma    P2     PM     17.9
Lmb    PM     PRI_B  8.5
Rme    PM     PRI_B  2.23k
Esec   S1     SEC_N  P2 PRI_B  {1/3.58}
Vsec   S1     S2     DC 0
Fpri   P2     PRI_B  Vsec  {1/3.58}
Rs1    S2     SEC_P  41.8
.ends NTE10_3

* L78L24 simplified model: ideal 24V regulator with dropout
* Input on IN, output on OUT, ground on GND
* Quiescent current: ~3mA (modeled as current source from IN to GND)
.subckt L78L24 IN OUT GND_PIN
Vreg  OUT  GND_PIN  24
Rout  IN   INT_IN   0.1
Iq    INT_IN GND_PIN 3m
.ends L78L24

* MMSZ5267B — 75V zener diode (DZ1 HV clamp since #121; it only protects)
.model MMSZ5267B D (Is=1n N=1 Rs=10 Cj0=50p Bv=75 Ibv=10u Vj=0.6 M=0.4)

* BZX55C68 — the 68V DZ1 before #121. Kept for the history section of docs/wcca.md
.model BZX55C68 D (Is=1n N=1 Rs=10 Cj0=50p Bv=68 Ibv=5m Vj=0.6 M=0.4)

* BAT54S — Schottky diode (each junction modeled individually)
* V_F ~0.2V at low-µA loads; used in active Dickson pump
* Parameters from op-txm-v3 / Vishay datasheet
.model BAT54S D (IS=1e-6 N=1.05 BV=100 RS=3)
